References: <1235575672.4360.42.camel@n61036.nbi.bbsrc.ac.uk> <33AC3729-28BD-47B0-974C-983BEB2E4EB8@r-project.org>
Message-ID: <1236693109.22544.68.camel@n61036.nbi.bbsrc.ac.uk>

Hi Simon,
Sorry for being unclear. I'll reword my message.

Is there a way to pass R objects, such as hclust objects between
JRI/Java classes (obviously, as some sort of Java/JRI data type). I have
a load of Java classes and methods that use JRI to carry out various
functions, to produce distance matrices, carry out clustering and
display the results graphically. I want to keep each step separate in
order for a user to choose which methods are performed next, so I have a
Java class which produces distance matrices, then I pass the distance
matrix to a Java class which does clustering, then I want to pass the
result of the clustering to a Java class which displays the clustering
graphically. All the classes use JRI. Sometimes I might want to go
straight from producing a distance matrix to displaying the result
graphically (eg a phylogenetic tree).
Now on to some code...
As an example, I'm using the Java method below which is passed the data
matrix and rownames of a distance method (such as 'euclidean' using the
dist{stats} method)
A 'JRIDataPack' is simply a Java class, made up of a double matrix
(containing the distance matrix) and a Java String array (containing row
names (which are accession names))


public JRIDataPack UPGMAClusterFromJRIDataPack (JRIDataPack list,
Rengine re, String method)
{
	//retrieve the data from the JRIDataPack
	double [][] matrix = list.getDoubleArray ();
	String [] rowArray = list.getStringArray ();
	int rows = matrix.length;
	int columns = matrix[0].length;

	//create an R matrix to insert the Java double matrix into
	re.eval ("r_matrix <- matrix(data = NA, nrow = "+rows+", ncol =
"+columns+", byrow = FALSE,dimnames = NULL)");

	//loop through the matrix and give the upgma_matrix the correct values
	for (int i = 0; i < rows; i++)
	{
	        re.eval ("i<- " +i);
	        for (int j = 0; j < columns; j++)
	        {
		        re.eval ("j<- " +j);
		        //R matrices start at index 1 (java at 0), so add 1 to current
position
		        re.eval ("ii <- i+1");
		        re.eval ("jj <- j+1");
		        //add values for the lower triangle..
		        re.eval ("r_matrix [ii,jj] <- "+matrix[i][j]);
	        }
	}
 	//get the names of the accessions from the [] rows array..
        String [] rowArray = list.getStringArray ();
        String rowString = JRIUtils.arrayToString (rowArray);
        //...and allocate the row names
        re.eval ("rownames(r_matrix) <- "+rowString);
        //make sure it's in the right format
        re.eval ("r_matrix <- as.matrix (r_matrix)");
        re.eval ("r_matrix <- as.dist (r_matrix)");
        //run hclust on the matrix
        re.eval ("clus_out <- hclust (r_matrix, method = '"+method
+"')");
        
        //get the n-1 by 2 matrix($merge). Row i of merge describes the
merging of clusters at step i of the clustering.
        REXP rexp = re.eval ("clus_out$merge");
        double [][] returnedMatrix = rexp.asDoubleMatrix ();
        
