You forgot to add . to the classpath, you should be using
java -cp JRI.jar;. rtest

In addition, you can have jri.dll in a separate directory if you set  
java.library.path to that directory, but there are pitfalls if I  
remember correctly - Java doesn't allow long paths so you would have  
to use the 8.3 notation. To sum up this is what will work anywhere  
(assuming your classes are in the current directory - if they are not,  
add the jar file to -cp):

set PATH=%PATH%;C:\PROGRA~1\R\R-28~1.0\bin
java -cp C:\PROGRA~1\R\R-28~1.0\library\rJava\jri\JRI.jar;. - 
Djava.library.path=C:\PROGRA~1\R\R-28~1.0\library\rJava\jri rtest


BTW: you can get the short path by running this in R:
cat(gsub("/","\\\\",system.file("jri",package="rJava")),"\n")


> Does this mean that any new Java class that implements JRI must be  
> put under jri\examples directory?
>

No.

